# Task 0 — Simple Web Stack

## Infrastructure overview

A user wants to access www.foobar.com. Here is what happens step by step,
and what every component in the infrastructure does.



## What is a server?

A server is a physical or virtual machine that provides services to other
computers (clients) over a network. In this infrastructure, a single server
hosts all components: the web server, the application server, the codebase,
and the database.

---

## What is the role of the domain name?

A domain name (foobar.com) is a human-readable alias for an IP address.
Instead of typing 8.8.8.8, users type www.foobar.com. The DNS system
translates that name into the IP address so the browser knows where to send
the request.

---

## What type of DNS record is "www" in www.foobar.com?

"www" is an A record. An A record maps a hostname directly to an IPv4 address.
Here, www.foobar.com → 8.8.8.8.

---

## What is the role of the web server?

The web server (Nginx) handles incoming HTTP/HTTPS connections from the user's
browser. It serves static files (HTML, CSS, images) directly, and forwards
dynamic requests to the application server.

---

## What is the role of the application server?

The application server executes the business logic of the application
(PHP-FPM, Gunicorn, etc.). It reads the codebase, processes the request,
queries the database if needed, and returns a response to the web server.

---

## What is the role of the database?

MySQL stores and retrieves persistent data (user accounts, posts, orders,
etc.). The application server queries it using SQL and uses the results to
build the response.

---

## What protocol does the server use to communicate with the user?

The server communicates with the user's browser over HTTP or HTTPS, which
runs on top of TCP/IP.

---

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SPOF (Single Point Of Failure)
There is only one server. If it goes down (hardware failure, crash, power
loss), the entire website becomes unavailable. There is no redundancy.

### Downtime during maintenance
Deploying new code requires restarting Nginx or the application server. During
that restart window, users get errors. There is no way to do a zero-downtime
deployment with a single server.

### Cannot scale with high traffic
A single server has a fixed amount of CPU and RAM. If traffic spikes, the
server gets overwhelmed and response times degrade or requests fail entirely.
There is no horizontal scaling possible in this setup.
